    "note": [
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "A technique is presented that modifies \"traditional\" curvature analysis of dome and fold structures by calculating strains and stresses directly from the principal curvatures of a bedding surface. In contrast to previous techniques in which various components of curvature have been used directly as a measure of fracture density, this technique is based on the fact that fractures form as a result of stress acting across a potential fracture plane and thus includes calculation of stresses as a result of doming or folding. Firstly, a method based on the First and Second Fundamental Forms of surfaces is presented that allows one to calculate principal curvatures from gridded 3-D data. Calculated curvatures are then used to compute strains that arise in strata due to pure bending, and then stretching strains are estimated from the changes in arc length of the strata. in the future stresses based on composite plate theory and certain interface conditions will be calculated from the total estimated strain and the constitutive properties of the rock. Perfect bonding and perfect slip between rock units during deformation represent two end member cases. A field study is proposed to evaluate the predictive potential of this approach. This research not only is of academic interest, but it directly bears on problems related to the evaluation and production of hydrocarbon reservoirs.",
            "type": "abstract",
            "identifier": [],
            "note": [],
            "appliesTo": []
        },
